## Process Rules

- Ask clarifying questions before assuming requirements
- Use dynamic code generators, not hard-coded static examples
- Always specify exact file name when writing code
- No temporary compatibility scaffolding: do not add aliases, typedef shims,
  temporary adapters, or "keep old and new in parallel" bridges
- If a change is too large for the current step, stop at a clear compile
  error instead of introducing temporary hacks that hide unfinished migration
- Prefer explicit breakage over hidden compatibility layers during planned
  architectural migrations
- Prefer explicit compile errors over stubs/placeholders when implementing
  migration steps. A visible broken contract is better than masked logic.

## Memory (hard rule)
- Memory is OFF. Do not persist anything to the harness memory store — no auto-save of facts, preferences, or project state.
- If something genuinely seems worth keeping across sessions, propose it to the user first. Only on explicit approval, write it into global config (a skill or an always-on rule) mirrored across all three harnesses — never into memory. See skill `harness-layout`.

## Incremental Development
For complex tasks (multiple files, architectural changes):
1. Analyze task thoroughly, create a detailed step-by-step plan
2. Present the plan to the user for review
3. Implement one step at a time — provide complete, ready-to-use code
4. Wait for user confirmation before proceeding
5. Answer clarifying questions before continuing

## Plans Handed Off to Another Agent
- When a plan will be executed by another AI agent autonomously (Claude Code/Codex, no chat context), write it at maximum detail: concrete AAA test cases, exact file paths, mock/class names, and a clear phased structure — not high-level summaries.

## Interaction Guidelines
- Assume user is familiar with programming concepts but may be new to Dart
- Explain Dart-specific features (null safety, futures, streams) when generating code
- When suggesting new dependencies, explain their benefits

## Post-Implementation Review
After completing each plan step:
1. Run `git diff` against the last commit
2. Perform a code review following the Code Review Checklist — analyze thoroughly
3. Fix any issues found
4. Only then stop and wait for the user's review

## Project Tooling
- Flutter is fvm-pinned; prefer `fvm flutter` / `fvm dart` over global
- Custom scripts (Python tooling, non-Dart helpers) live in `scripts/`, not `tool/`
- Heavy Gradle builds are disk/RAM-hungry — clean up after yourself, confirm before a build

## File Tools (hard rule)
- Read files with the file-read tool, edit with the file-edit/write tool
- Never read via `cat`/`sed -n`/`head`; never edit via `sed -i`, heredoc or an inline `python3 -` script
- Shell is for *running* things — tests, analyze, format, codegen, `git status`/`diff` — plus content search (`grep`/`find`) when the harness has no search tool
- This overrides any harness mode that suggests editing files through shell commands

## Package Management
- Use `mcp_dart_pub` if available; otherwise `flutter pub add <package>`
- Dev dependencies: `flutter pub add dev:<package>`
- Remove: `dart pub remove <package>`
- Search packages: `mcp_dart_pub_dev_search` first; explain benefits when suggesting new deps
